  "type": "reading",
  "title": "Passage Two · Delaying Retirement",
  "subtitle": "美国老年人推迟退休的原因",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "source": "2025年12月大学英语四级考试真题（第1套）· Section C · Passage Two",
  "instructions": "Questions 51 to 55 are based on the following passage.",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "en": "Katharine Abraham, an economics professor, was chatting with her hairdresser (理发师) about retirement plans. The economist said she plans to continue working because she wants to. The hairdresser agreed but for a different reason: She needs the money.",
      "zh": "经济学教授凯瑟琳·亚伯拉罕和她的理发师聊起了退休计划。这位经济学家说，她打算继续工作，因为她自己愿意。理发师也赞同继续工作，但理由不同：她需要这份收入。"
    },
    {
      "en": "Both scenarios (情况) are contributing to a big increase in the number of people in the U.S. working into their 70s. Over the past 20 years, the share of Americans working in their 70s has risen from less than 10% to nearly 15%.",
      "zh": "这两种情况共同促使美国70多岁仍在工作的人数大幅增加。过去20年间，美国70多岁人群中仍在工作的比例已从不足10%上升至近15%。"
    },
    {
      "en": "In addition to people being healthier and living much longer, economists say that a combination of financial considerations such as years of slow rise in real wages and a shift away from traditional pensions in the private sector are some of the reasons people delay retirement. The decline of manufacturing and the increase in the number of people working in less labor-intensive occupations also has contributed to the trend, says Abraham, who researches work and retirement decisions of older Americans.",
      "zh": "经济学家指出，除了人们身体更健康、寿命更长外，多种经济因素也是人们推迟退休的部分原因，例如实际工资多年来增长缓慢，以及私营部门逐渐不再采用传统退休金制度。研究美国老年人就业与退休决策的亚伯拉罕表示，制造业的衰退，以及从事劳动强度较低职业的人数增加，也促成了这一趋势。"
    },
    {
      "en": "“Which matters more depends on what your history up until that point has been in the type of work you’re doing,” Abraham says.",
      "zh": "亚伯拉罕说：“究竟哪种因素影响更大，取决于你在此之前从事这类工作的经历。”"
    },
    {
      "en": "The overall trend is hitting Americans of all different levels of educational attainment, although the percentages vary by category. The share of Americans with bachelor’s degrees who were working into their 70s reached nearly 20% in 2018. For those with a high school degree or less, the proportion of those working in their 70s had risen to around 10%, while those with some college education were in the middle at around 15%.",
      "zh": "这一总体趋势影响着不同教育水平的美国人，尽管各类人群的比例有所不同。2018年，拥有学士学位的美国人中，70多岁仍在工作的比例达到近20%。对于高中及以下学历者，这一比例已上升至约10%；接受过部分大学教育的人群则处于中间水平，约为15%。"
    },
    {
      "en": "Martin Neil Baily, an economist who is leading a research project on retirement security, notes that quitting a career can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, particularly for men. He suggests that many college-educated workers are choosing to stay in the labor force more for social benefits than for financial reasons. They’re also more likely to be in professional occupations where they tend to enjoy their work more.",
      "zh": "经济学家马丁·尼尔·贝利正主持一项关于退休保障的研究项目。他指出，结束职业生涯可能使人产生疏离感与孤独感，对男性而言尤其如此。他认为，许多受过大学教育的劳动者选择留在劳动力市场，更多是为了获得社交方面的益处，而非出于经济原因。他们也更可能从事专业性职业，在这类工作中往往更能获得乐趣。"
    },
    {
      "en": "Workers in more physical jobs, meanwhile, may be more likely to look forward to retiring, Baily says, suggesting those who stay on are more likely doing so for financial reasons.",
      "zh": "与此同时，贝利表示，从事体力要求较高工作的人或许更盼望退休；而那些选择继续工作的人，更可能是出于经济原因。"
    }
  ],
